Black carbon surface forcing: atmospheric BC + sea-ice BC.

all deposited black carbon is treated as refractory dissolved organic carbon.

ATM_BLACK_CARBON_FLUX is a sum of BCDEPWET,   BCPHIDRY, and BCPHODRY from the CESM coupler in kg/m^2/s and converted to g/cm^2/s using 0.1 and the active surface ocean mask (RCALCT). So no BC over land cells. 
  The forcing file is from aerosoldep_WACCM.ensmean_monthly_hist_1849-2015_0.9x1.25_CMIP6_c180926.nc (sandbox/inputdata/atm/cam/chem/trop/aero)
    cd sandbox/inputdata/atm/cam/chem/trop/aero
    cd /glade/campaign/cesm/cesmdata/inputdata/atm/cam/chem/trop_mozart_aero/aero

Atmospheric BC =  hydrophilic dry BC + hydrophobic dry BC  + hydrophilic wet BC

In addition, black carbon from sea ice (SEAICE_BLACK_CARBON_FLUX) is the same calculation. But the bcpho and bcphi are calculated during the run and has no NC forcing file. 
  Sea-ice BC = hydrophobic BC from sea ice + hydrophilic BC from sea ice
Liu et al., 2012: The volume-mean size for BC and POM emissions is 0.134 µm diameter non-hygroscopic particle. Dissolved fraction is <0.2 µm. Thus, I partition all aerosol BC in the dissolved fraction for DOCr. Aerosol BC is DOCr because it is characterized as aromatic condensed material. https://gmd.copernicus.org/articles/5/709/2012/ 

Summary: my code takes atmospheric and sea-ice BC deposition supplied by CESM, converts it to a carbon molar surface flux, 
assumes all of that deposited BC carbon behaves as refractory dissolved organic carbon, 
adds it directly to MARBL's DOCr surface flux, 
and lets POP convert that surface flux into a first-layer volumetric DOCr tendency through its standard vertical tracer-flux divergence calculation; 
a parallel 6%-Fe assumption adds BC-associated iron to the ocean Fe forcing.


